      "sha256": "e0afb5c187aca1e075f9549153b1e896320bb0539544f66d3293fab4b4b77c92",
      "reason": "The whole task is an anomaly index relative to a monthly climatology baseline plus the ONI 3-month running-mean criterion, so this fragment fixes the baseline definition (1991-2020 vs 1981-2010 offset), the prohibition on mixing DJF/JFM seasons when forming 3-month means, and event/persistence rules.",
      "notes_cut": 1,
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "The anomaly must come from the field's **own** 1991-2020 monthly climatology (`compute_anomaly_vs_own_climatology`), each month differenced against its own calendar month so the seasonal cycle is removed; state the climatology and `base_period` used — the tools record both in the result's attrs.",
        "The RECON_8110 minus RECON_9120 difference is a baseline effect (one constant offset per calendar month, variability identical), not a reconstruction error; likewise the published 1981-2010 minus 1991-2020 offset — report both as unweighted means over the months."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "common-comparison-mask",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/common-comparison-mask.md",
      "sha256": "bbb98200f52b4a692ce097704b8c438179a795a44bec6b856c03cf31931af4ea",
      "reason": "The correlation, RMSE and largest deviation between RECON_9120 and the CPC Nino 3.4 anomaly exist only over months where both series have data, so the comparison must be restricted to their common coverage.",
      "status": "not_applicable",
      "not_applicable_reason": "the comparison here is between two time series of one regional index, not a cell-by-cell field metric, so no shared grid mask is built; the offsets and correlations are unweighted over months, not over cells."
    },
    {
      "id": "serial-correlation-and-significance",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/serial-correlation-and-significance.md",
      "sha256": "fe11bd008257a589f44e369faba5fa31ab3cf9ad99280e88c763ac45d2b0e917",
      "reason": "RECON and CPC are strongly autocorrelated monthly series, so the correlation reported to six decimals has an overstated naive sample size and any significance or agreement statement must account for that.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Correlate RECON_9120 with the CPC 1991-2020 Nino 3.4 series as they are (`detrend=False`): two estimates of the same quantity, whose shared trend is part of the agreement being checked, not a spurious link.",
        "The monthly series is strongly autocorrelated, so any significance quoted must use the effective sample size the tools apply by default (`analyze_correlation`, `fit_1D_trend`), with the unadjusted values reported alongside — never the naive step count."
      ]
    }
